#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134658} :activity/id #uuid "cf31c13c-070a-552d-91a4-1a67e47f24d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135043 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors ![](images/b9/b986146bf3d9edbf85c9047aa0e0acac54b9dd962c5c1f1b2d1612a36444337b.svg) Can we find a *non-zero* vector ![](images/2b/2be010cc1a39d762cb78272a1c6f8b10574823fa549ba2e54066f15c1d312ff9.svg) that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n![](images/4a/4aee36f32445ae5fe5286d7a5aa7600f1f7fc8449fb981509cbd999822408c90.svg)\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7fff6c9-9086-5eef-b56a-a87ba95ad93b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dd572f85-b48d-5bec-90bb-6618a7fc0fc2" :step/math-academy-id 20219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133587 :question/id #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" :question/math-academy-id "e-5560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431859}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"} :step/id #uuid "74cfe2c6-a806-59db-9604-de65c6177317" :step/math-academy-id 20220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133588 :question/id #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418769}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"} :step/id #uuid "64ed6cee-8c85-5d4d-97ee-35ae15ad1e64" :step/math-academy-id 20221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135044 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\n  \n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector in $H.$\n\nTo check whether a vector belongs to $H^{\\perp},$ we use the following theorem:\n\n*A vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if it is orthogonal to a set of vectors that spans $H$. *\n\nIn other words:\n\n*If $H=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\},$ then $\\mathbf{v} \\in H^{\\perp}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\}.$*\n\nFor example, if the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2,$ then the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of $H$ consists of all vectors that are perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8cf96c9871966fb0a7a921b70229e4a17bb9f6dad1756986f0500c70be6ab95.png)\n\n  \n\nSome other important things to notice about $H^{\\perp}$ are as follows:\n\n- $H^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\n- $(H^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = H$\n\n- $H \\cap H^{\\perp} = \\{\\mathbf 0\\}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "565f1aa6-6d92-5a0d-8035-62690c9f8e0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3617 :tutorial/title "Orthogonal Complements"} :step/id #uuid "261dd9cf-9401-5924-9e13-578c45a64439" :step/math-academy-id 20222 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133589 :question/id #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" :question/math-academy-id "e-5562"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512878}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"} :step/id #uuid "c64ae3ad-aece-5b13-99de-d1ff9cb5fbfd" :step/math-academy-id 20223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133632 :question/id #uuid "b7cb0b20-e2a6-56d7-b568-ae08c259e581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431858}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "cad6aad8-f1f1-51f6-82ae-549193d8d909" :step/math-academy-id 21872 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134662}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133989}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418774} :activity/id #uuid "127d383e-0561-515b-b9bf-949bec0e816d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596addf6-8054-5598-9e84-8da605078378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418746} {:answer/id #uuid "78313721-3417-534e-bd9d-35344d3b992b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418747} {:answer/id #uuid "896cdb45-076d-5afe-9612-da56ea5a4872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418749} {:answer/id #uuid "90b1dfb5-99df-5a22-8ca3-79658b3f711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418750} {:answer/id #uuid "c979a875-601a-5ca5-ac66-79e409fb9e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418746} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bf075f-8e0c-5f11-88cc-cf746d21f00c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e54a7c62-bee3-5a5a-868c-0fd63367a8c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80525" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b+c}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b+c}=\\frac{3}{2+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0577a2b9-4489-58d5-ac62-a197003874f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4730af-deb8-5685-8db4-054a0f26476b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418737} {:answer/id #uuid "45409e4f-d6f7-583a-a654-f6549650ec01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418740} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf16aae-72e1-5107-ae16-5b805fc64b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418742} {:answer/id #uuid "510cc82f-89b0-512e-a913-16359c5c9470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7982ef7-967e-53d6-ac5f-6caebe695c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71e16f6-f24e-58ff-9688-d083942b3ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95765da4-bd97-53db-8498-26a824409464" :question/math-academy-id "q-80524" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "35869f4e-ea24-507d-8562-2faa7b32b71c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2560798b-15eb-58b9-9b21-90ea11e0e02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418738} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd54508-dbfe-5bf5-8588-0629d075c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418739} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e698f-5a53-5e88-a9d0-bf26c7dc0622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418741} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b5d004-ff0d-52e0-ae59-f92ef8861bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418756} {:answer/id #uuid "d3984660-76a2-5389-8742-ab715d50f835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418757} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7279778-d43a-559a-840e-ae02e897e9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a115f8b-a248-5ebc-93b2-6b42bb9d7f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-140470" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}).$\n So, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"} :step/id #uuid "76d46c90-8a7d-5c38-905d-f11edade0d5a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c78237-7ba8-5f5d-a33b-f0e13b1bcaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454048} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ae988e-f035-54a7-a23f-739a60fc9826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454048} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a1a93b-7a1e-598e-9ac9-1fec0a8541af" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1121815-b4cb-5a43-b5a6-e6c5f54f549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454050} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc194be-1802-5b3c-9ead-0634aaac6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454051} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c705186-a967-52c8-8f4b-6be1b77a56ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac97a943-76b0-577f-974e-60a75af70b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454052} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3399ef-8ee6-5f98-9847-2e8f4d1a6f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454052} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18f9b5e-6144-516e-bca1-6e1944599bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac83e377-f632-58f6-8bed-4004335968a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80514" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **II.** $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **III.** $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=-8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7791ae0e-534a-5b36-b058-c53eaf417327" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5210b26c-a0e7-5a21-a878-831f0c686a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418744} {:answer/id #uuid "541db5fa-5828-53cc-9410-f2d882f278c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418745} {:answer/id #uuid "8938062f-6293-5274-8504-3f32097a764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418748} {:answer/id #uuid "fb610657-ef5d-596b-a8a0-847fe72dc416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418760} {:answer/id #uuid "fc792583-9e07-55f0-920d-d217098932ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e9ece4-46c5-5719-8b0a-2409c92f79a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49bf04a8-ac22-532b-bad0-4061fdca5fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39624" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c995944d-1a26-5669-9909-6a019a785d1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418773}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418736}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512968} :activity/id #uuid "fadfdee6-b9d7-5ac9-b37e-51103fa03a0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315b71a4-28e0-5aea-b2c4-c7a46375dded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324369} {:answer/id #uuid "39895333-f8bd-5aaa-90e7-ecbd25f291f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324438} {:answer/id #uuid "70b0d670-feb2-5e76-a4a2-11b302ca3fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324898} {:answer/id #uuid "821b82f3-fe56-570f-ade4-2d7232dcbfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325065} {:answer/id #uuid "f75310d1-47c6-57cf-9ba0-7e4f001d4215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324898} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64c6253-854b-5bb4-95cc-01aebed6b16b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17bd69d7-65e4-535e-bfc3-813ca9368a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-39915" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "26a546ba-54ab-5cc8-83e6-b006c83a2260" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842b5c5c-cc5c-50d2-b823-6def6fca04e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133033} {:answer/id #uuid "da8821b3-5d06-5e21-80f5-a553896d64c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133034} {:answer/id #uuid "15e8b357-5098-57b9-8c74-02a2282b9714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133035} {:answer/id #uuid "38f6361e-a902-560f-9217-4c195d638701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133036} {:answer/id #uuid "660803b9-f7f4-568d-8a7c-5d45e4320d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133037}] :answer-field/id #uuid "142bdc42-c623-5b18-9590-d0d791655eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae2689b-03da-5b4a-8bd9-e03a7a3d536c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393086} {:answer/id #uuid "2c523036-0297-53fb-8cf4-0a2347f12c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393087} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ddb235-9955-5a27-87c5-3f2c81216912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393088} {:answer/id #uuid "56996c07-885b-5b0d-8973-e1ca5a8ac3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393089} {:answer/id #uuid "81374ca6-2fbe-5ee7-a0ba-75942ffe6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393088} :answer-field/id #uuid "98103ba7-7941-593f-8c55-fa6f21186d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e055065d-3cdf-5f06-8b7f-a22a1c681c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-71102" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "5ab17b99-dd7c-5473-a02e-1887d75473a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc41dc0-e625-54ec-935c-e2218ad7619b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512129} {:answer/id #uuid "23439808-c020-5fe6-9947-75475ae07c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512176} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a43a48-c5a3-5fe0-b7e7-03cf00683fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\text{0}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512496} {:answer/id #uuid "bd28a383-d220-5ede-95f6-48a1f6e0c3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-7{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512554} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b37a3-f596-55cd-9709-b88fbdabcc2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512593} :answer-field/id #uuid "356e4ab9-2091-593a-99dd-55afcb98c941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b5b5791-1037-533d-a2f0-5ffb4bac0dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140469" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-14{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6102b09c-519b-52a5-908c-ac2692fae51a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e627cc-9ad9-5364-a03f-0a60a7ff7e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345685} {:answer/id #uuid "35a5cdc8-809a-5ac7-966a-3468c85a0cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345814} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad54b60-cd95-5301-a3d6-c6926d9c9dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346126} {:answer/id #uuid "628879f1-3e6f-5f07-bd70-ca54cc5545bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346212} {:answer/id #uuid "7cffd9e3-8a81-51c0-af55-dcb26b21e161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345685} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222f2a3-c047-5414-b19b-01d3b4f258cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff28c518-d713-5726-9541-4836cde2e6b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80539" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=5$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{-2+5}{-1}=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "b50cddb9-32fe-5feb-88b9-dbdd94647008" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d3f947-88e4-5988-8e0d-3fd68cbe30be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345410} {:answer/id #uuid "2f715520-d0fb-53f3-b206-534321c0bc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345771} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd3071-12ba-5752-be59-a530d172b957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346101} {:answer/id #uuid "b7805445-b42b-5871-918b-c9f63736a062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346951} {:answer/id #uuid "c9365c52-9d6b-5c2f-bb15-037396c3ab84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345771} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15bb1c1-681b-5425-b17c-5396ce1536d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "105935bf-05fc-5973-9fd9-74c435a6a9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80581" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $h=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ A vector  $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$   \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =4\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-4)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-4)=30\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)=-19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$  only\"."} :step/id #uuid "b668892f-4bf5-5939-a70a-e3c5d414340b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512990}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512095}]}
